From: Claude <claude.ai.2026@simonlaux.de>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 22:20:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 74/74] web-app: slim under-navbar player strip on mobile;
 dedupe mini-player sender line

The global voice player lived at the bottom of the chat-list column, and
on the single-column layout that column is `display: none` while a chat
is open -- so on a phone the player vanished the moment you opened a chat
and there was no way to pause, change speed or jump back to the message
(#137). Playback kept going from a surface nobody could see.

On that layout the player now renders as a slim strip pinned under the
navbar, mounted in MainScreen *outside* both columns so it survives the
chat-list/chat-view switch and is visible in both. One row, 36px: the
play/pause button driving the singleton, the truncated "sender . chat"
label (tap = the same jump-to-message as the bar, and no dead button when
there is nothing to jump to), the speed pill on the shared
ctx.playbackRate, and close. Under it a 2px hairline -- played portion in
--colorPrimary, track in --navBarButtonActive -- fed by the singleton's
currentTime/duration. It is a position readout, not a control: the bubble
owns the waveform and seeking, the OS media session owns scrubbing from
outside the app, and a 2px pointer target would be a bad third option, so
the hairline is aria-hidden and has no interaction. The strip continues
the navbar's own colors (--navBarBackground/--navBarText, not the
always-dark account-sidebar scheme the bar uses) because it sits in the
content column.

The strip's 38px band is reserved, not overlaid: it is positioned
absolutely (z-index 2 -- above the message list's local scopes, below the
toast at 100 and dialogs), and a sibling-combinator rule keyed off the
strip's presence gives both small-screen navbars a 38px margin-bottom, so
the scrolling areas below them simply start lower. Overlaying instead
would hide the topmost chat-list row / message and, worse, swallow taps
meant for it. The strip only exists on the small-screen layout, so with
no strip in the DOM the rule matches nothing and layout is unchanged.

One surface at a time. `voicePlayerSurface(smallScreen, customControls,
hasSrc)` in mediaSession.ts is the single decision: none / native (setting
off, unchanged on every viewport) / strip (small screen) / bar (wide).
Both mount points render `GlobalVoiceMessagePlayer`; the one that is not
the current surface returns null *and* keeps its hooks idle, so the sender
fetch, the jump wiring and the media-session registration never run twice
-- the media session belongs to whichever instance is mounted, and a
breakpoint change hands it over cleanly (React flushes all effect
cleanups before the setups). Four mocha cases pin the table.

Independently, the bar's sender line no longer repeats itself: in a 1:1
chat the chat is named after the contact, so it read "Bob Martinez / Bob
Martinez". The chat sub-line now shows only when it differs from the
sender name, which keeps "Me / Saved Messages" intact and applies to the
strip's label too. Truncation and aria are unchanged.

Everything stays behind experimentalAudioPlayerControls: with the setting
off the native re-parented <audio> bar is byte-identical on all viewports.
CustomAudioControls' element-state subscription is extracted as
`useAudioElementState` so the strip reuses it instead of copying the
paused/time/duration mirror; the strip is its own component so its ~4Hz
timeupdate re-renders don't reach the player's fetch hooks.
